  "response": "Separating a modeling failure from a communication failure begins with the archived forecast record. The Storm Prediction Center issues convective outlooks that assign categorical risk levels. The terminology in those products is fixed: \"high risk\" is the highest categorical risk level used in Storm Prediction Center convective outlooks. For the event under review, the record admits exactly one of two accounts, and the panel's first task is to establish which: either the Storm Prediction Center issued a high risk outlook on the morning of the outbreak, or the Storm Prediction Center never raised the outlook above enhanced risk on the day of the outbreak, one of the two being the case and both together impossible.\n\nResponsibility for the wording and timing of warning text depends on which office held the pen. Tornado warnings are issued by local National Weather Service forecast offices. The Storm Prediction Center issues all tornado warnings for the United States. Warning authority exercised county by county from the local forecast offices cannot be squared with every tornado warning in the country originating from a single national desk, so a review panel resolves that question of issuing authority before it apportions blame for message content, lead time, or polygon size.\n\nAttribution to the guidance itself rests on grid resolution and on what resolution purchases in a tornadic environment. The High-Resolution Rapid Refresh model runs on a 3-kilometer grid, whereas the Global Forecast System is run at 1-kilometer grid spacing. Reviewers rerun and score both archived forecasts against radar-derived storm reports, comparing updraft helicity tracks, the timing of convective initiation, the depth of the simulated cold pool, and the modeled low-level shear against proximity soundings.\n\nEven though numerical weather prediction models cannot forecast the location of an individual tornado days in advance, convection-allowing model guidance underestimated the intensity of the outbreak. The explanatory burden then falls on at least one of two deficiencies, and perhaps on both: that underestimation of intensity in the convection-allowing guidance, and the finding that warning messages failed to reach a substantial share of residents in the affected counties. Residents must opt in to receive tornado warnings through Wireless Emergency Alerts, and that opt-in requirement produced the shortfall in message reach documented by door-to-door surveys and call-center logs. Identification of the guidance error came first in the sequence of events; the agency's formal review record followed afterward. That the National Weather Service published a service assessment of the April 2011 tornado outbreak confirms that the agency conducts formal service assessments after high-impact weather events.",
